> The best review of all this is:
> 
>     Bennett, C. (1990) "The Thermodynamics of Computation - a Review",
>     in Maxwell's Demon - Entropy, Information, Computing, ed. H. Leff, 
>     A. Rex, Princeton Univ. Press. pp. 213-248.

I've never found Leff's book but I have an earlier article with the same
or a similar title by Bennett, from a journal.  Can anyone provide a cite
for this article?  It might be more available.  (I won't have access to
it until Sat. or Sun.)

One quick comment on a phrase Tim made -

> any large computations that reach "new" conclusions--presumably the reason
> to do computations in the first place

It's an interesting semantic question as to whether any computer, especially
a reversible one, can reach "new" conclusions.  In a sense, anything that
comes out of a computer is _implicit_ in its input (counting both the data
and program (and hardware design, I suppose) as part of the input).  The
computer can only throw away information, it can't create it.  The information
content of its output can be no greater than that of its input.

One way to put this would be to say that you already knew the results of
the program, you just didn't know you knew them.  I remember a Socratic
dialogue where Socrates determined that someone didn't know the right answer
to a question, then by asking him (leading) questions he was able to have
the subject deduce the right answer.  Socrates made a similar point, that
the person had known the right answer all along, but he didn't know he knew
it.  I think Socrates used this as a proof of reincarnation, but that's not
really relevent, I suppose.

Another question is where new information comes from.  List old-timers will
remember Kenneth "Tom" Tolman who loved to argue this point, and had
elaborated it into a rather crankish theory about the meaning of life.
But I do think Tom had a point in arguing that new information could only
arise non-deterministically, because deterministic calculations can't create
information.

I'm still hoping to someday find Leff's and Cover's books to improve my
understanding of these issues.

Hal

