   X-Name: Timothy C. May

   Concentration on IQs, past IQ achievements, and techniques for increasing
   IQ (usually via superficial self-help books that emphasize such nonsense as
   speed reading and rote memorization of trivia) is an "entropic" trend, in
   my opinion.

   X-Name: Robin Hanson 

   I'm quite curious about what
   percentage in the variance in achievement can be explained by variance
   in intrinsic characteristics of a person at a certain point in time,
   as opposed to the variance in the world the live in, and the
   opportunities they were given.  I suspect that the later variance is
   by far the largest contribution, but I could be wrong.

   X-Name: Tom Morrow

   But semi-seriously, uberfriends, I have known plenty of supposedly high-IQ
   folks who seem to specialize in helpless floundering and plenty of poor
   test-takers who have gripped realspace by the short curlies.  I have thus
   concluded that IQ tests serve best at testing test-taking ability (though
   I'm not denying they can serve other ends).  I'll take good company and
   gumption over big numbers any day.

What I've seen written about IQ tests is that they *do* measure
*something*, because all IQ-type tests correlate quite strongly, and
correlate strongly with the grades one gets in school.  However, they
don't correlate with how much money you make, life satisfaction, or
any other measure of "life success" that anybody's devised.

As Tom says, people with low IQs can often grasp life by the short and
curlies.  I suspect that (in the relatively open culture of the US),
success is more due to motivation, persistance, discipline, and other
practical skills than opportunity as such.  You can almost always get
enough opportunity, if you notice it when it's around and grab it with
gusto.

