Date: Fri, 2 Apr 93 16:43:46 -0800
From: cappello%cs@hub.ucsb.edu (Peter Cappello)
Subject: organizations

>You can't eliminate the problem of management
>or organization by turning the firm into a market. This is the point
>I'm trying to make, however ineptly.

(BTW, I would like to apologize for my strident remarks about  
employees.  My context was Extropia, but I doubt I made that clear.)

The firm would not be a market.  It would be composed of for-profit  
organizations.  One makes use of the market in these organizations at  
the time that one [re]negotiates a contract.

>Why does turning employees into contractors solve decision  
>conflicts?  Why wouldn't having closely held stock (one owner)  
>suffice? 


There are 2 issues here: decision-making and results-orientation.
The employee-as-contractor approach deals with the latter issue.
Conceptually, an employee is a degenerate kind of contractor  
(neglecting current tax distinctions).  The idea is for business  
owners to become more results-oriented.  I believe that taking the  
notion of delegation to its logical conclusion leads to paying people  
only for results.  Once you start paying only for results, you have  
entered the realm of contracting (in the non-degenerate sense). It  
seems to me that increasing the degree of delegation (i.e.,  
results-orientation) is analogous, in system design, to increasing  
encapsulation.

Regarding the former issue, decision-making, Browne's advice is to  
have only 1 person responsible for each decision.  When 2 people are  
involved, there may be no decision that is optimal with respect to  
each person's value system. (There have been discussions on this list  
how to proceed in some of these cases.)  Hence, partnerships almost  
always lead to messy personal conflicts.  This situation, extended to  
Boards of Directors, and beyond, is commonly called corporate  
politics.  Not a pretty sight.  There may exist sophisticated ways to  
proceed: To find a "best fit" solution with respect to the distinct  
value systems.  If such procedures exist, I believe that they would  
have a market flavor to them.  Again, Browne's (brute force) solution  
to this problem is to structure the organization so that there is  
only 1 person who has the authority/responsibility for any given  
decision.

Please clarify what you mean by closely held stock.  Sorry.

-Pete
