Date: Sat, 03 Apr 93 06:02:03 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: TECH: Entropic costs of computation

One point Derek Zahn made in his original post about limits of
computation is the (subjective) nature of macrostates etc which doesn't
jibe well with the (objective) heat produced by computing.  How can a
subjective measure of uncertainty, like entropy (read: log no. of
macrostates), be used to calculate objective things like the production
of heat during a computation?  

The answer is heat, like entropy, is a [cringe] subjective phenomenon,
dependent on the level of knowledge about a system.  The level of
interest in a system defines the labels for the macrostate.  The entropy
of the system will scale according to the log of the number of
microstates that are compatible with the macrostate.  So two different
people can have two different measures of entropy for the same thing. 
The more closely you scrutinise something the more you pin down the
state and the lower the entropy associated with the system (in your
mind), because the fewer internal degrees of motion there are. 
Similarly with heat.  What a disinterested observer perceives as heat
may, to a more perspicacious observer, be cunningly encoded information.

When Derek says that we can, in principle, know everything about a
computer he has defined his level of interest as nanoscopic.  At this
level of definition the entropy of the computer is zero and does not
increase as it performs calculations.  Our near-perfect knowledge of the
computer makes it a reversible computer (provided we also have the
technological abilities to reverse all the component momenta).  But
neither does it produce 'heat'.  The concept has no meaning at the
nanoscale.  Instead of heat we record the individual motions of zillions
of atoms.

In the example Hal used where we are erasing the contents of register
A, reg A ==> 0, then the uncertainty about the original contents of A
have been transferred somewhere else (it doesn't matter where).  If we
don't know what A was then the state of 'elsewhere' is now unknown and
this transfer of uncertainty can be interpreted as the release of heat
(=nano- level uncertainty).  If the state of A was known (say reg A =
1)  then we can make a more precise statement about the effect of
clearing down A on it's environment and no entropy has been released
into reg A's environment.

