Subject: I**2:  Brain Tuning (getting smart the old fashioned way)

Well let's get to it...

IQ is not meaningless any more than "computer benchmarks" are
meaningless.  A benchmark does not indicate how other "applications"
will necessarily perform but a properly designed one will yield
"useful" information.  To approach any problem in optimization we must
have a method to measure *that* which we are optimizing for.  In this
case-- intelligence.  Those of you who are "off the scale" for standard
IQ tests must seek some other measurement tool (Lefty, Eric).  An
individual test problem may draw on several mental skills to one degree
or another to be solved.  We might consider a ~good~ IQ test a
"representative set" of skills required to solve "real world"
problems.  Alternatively, we might consider an achievement or "mastery
test" a "representative set" of skills and knowledge required to
complete some project.

When I begin to optimize an application I do some "profiling" and seek
to identify the "bottle-necks" in the system (forgive my use of CS
terminology, I'm sure that instructional psychologists have their own
words for this).  I am usually dealing with "well-understood" systems.
Black-Box optimization is somewhat different however.  This is how I
would characterize my understanding of my own brain at this time.  By
"tweaking" the various mental skills we can observe what influence they
have on the measured result-- IQ.  From this we can draw conclusions
about the importance of certain skills and their relative contribution
in overall intelligence.  This approach is intuitively appealing to me
as I have had success with it in dealing with many "undocumented"
systems.

In earlier postings on this subject I refered to Marilyn vos Savant's book
and do so here again (at my reputation's peril, no doubt).

Savant, Marilyn vos., Fleischer, Leonore., _Brain Building_, Bantam Book, 1990.

Therein she identifies the 15 catagories of the Stanford-Binet
intelligence test for adults as (p. 27):

Vocabulary			Repeating Digits Reversed
Codes				Sentence Building

... ( ok I won't type the whole list )

Her book then goes into exercises for improving ("tweaking"?)
performance in those "catagories".  It should be noted that tweaking
can mean suppression or expression of some mental skill.  Another word,
"frobbing", is what one does when they don't know what knob they're
playing with or what the consequences are (thanks Quinn).

On page 23, she seems to take exception with the Dr. John Ertl and Dr.
Bernard Elliot referenced in John McPherson's posting dated today, April
3 (thanks John M for the timely post). I quote from Marilyn:

	They [Drs. Ertl and Bernard] have devised an entirely new way
	of testing the intelligence quotient, a test that is
	unconcerned with what the subject has learned in school or out
	of it.  All the testing subject has to do is look at a light.

	Wearing electrodes on his or her skull that pick up brain
	waves, the person being tested is hooked up to a computer that
	amplifies the waves, shows them on an oscilloscope, and flashes
	a light every second for two minutes.  The computer then
	records how quickly the eye--therefore the brain--responds to
	the light.  That's all there is to the test which Drs. Elliot
	and Ertl claim is entirely objective, and a true reading of the
	ability to learn.

I tend to agree with Marilyn on this point.  I would not want to draw
conclusions about the "utility" of an application (meme?) running on a
computer by measuring it's "ping time".  (h'mmm I rather like that word--
"utility").  Brain utility is ultimately what I'm trying to increase not
IQ.  Increasing IQ for IQ sake is something to impress (or bore) your
friends with.  Kind of like body building ["beep-beep" digression alert!]

More at some future date.  Sorry to leave a thread just when it's
getting started but I must now apply my limited brain power to some
"real world" problems.  (deadlines approaching)

-Ed Lane

