>> When Derek says that we can, in principle, know everything about a
>> computer he has defined his level of interest as nanoscopic.  At this
>> level of definition the entropy of the computer is zero and does not
>> increase as it performs calculations.  Our near-perfect knowledge of
>> the computer makes it a reversible computer (provided we also have
>> the technological abilities to reverse all the component momenta). 
>> But neither does it produce 'heat'.  The concept has no meaning at
>> the nanoscale.  Instead of heat we record the individual motions of
>> zillions of atoms.
> 
> The 'ah ha!' light finally went on when I read this paragraph of
> yours.  If we design the computer so that the position of every atom
> has meaning, then regardless of what computation we do, we
> generate no heat by definition.  We generate heat only when we dispose
> of information /outside the well defined environment of the computing
> machinery/.  Only when we throw information away into the environment,
> our heat sink.

Precisely what I was trying to say!

>               If I'm understanding this correctly, reversibility is
> important because once we've done our no-heat computation, if we want
> to do further computation we either have to reverse the original
> computation to get us back to a useful state, or dump heat into the
> environment to get us back to a useful state.

And even computers that seem irreversible at the macroscale are
reversible in reality (on the nano-scale).
> 
> Robin was right (assuming I've got it): really simple once you see it.
> Thanks!
> 
> [..]
> david             david@staff.udc.upenn.edu

The point I want to expand on is:  I see Drexler's "machine phase" not
as a collect of gears, rods and switches banging into each other, but
rather as some supermolecule.  A molecule not just of a few thousand or
millions of daltons but of trillions of trillions of daltons.  Like some
Victorian engineer let loose with a mechano/ tinker-toy set, the
"Machine phase" conjures up images of conventional engines, producing
heat and friction.  The supermolecule image lends itself more to the
image of a precise mechanism (like say the chlorophyll molecule, but a
*lot* bigger).  Most of the information processing is performed by
electron transfer between branches of the (relatively stationary)
molecule, without the nasty phonon creation that occurs when rigid
atomic arrays bash into each other.

I believe that the velocity-dependent entropy limitations Drexler gives
in Nanosystems may be too restrictive.  They apply to the (classical)
rod logic machines that he has outlined, perhaps.  But do they apply to
supermolecules, whose internals are based on (quantum) nanoelectronics? 
It interesting that the velocity-dependent entropy calculations, based
on extrapolating formulae for classical physics, break down for his
potential well merging scenarios.  There the entropy/energy dissipation
is strictly related to information-theoretic considerations.

I suspect that the models people currently have of reversible computers
having to run very, very slowly to reduce energy dissipation is way, way
too pessimistic.  Heat dissipation in (quantum) nano-electrocomputer the
will be purely governed by information loss which, for a reversible
computer, is zero.

Perhaps, to speculate more wildly, there will be a state analogous to
superconductivity in the supermolecule.  Once we reach a certain level
of design fidelity the supermolecules will operate without noticeable
frictive losses.

